<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vale of Glamorgan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vale of Glamorgan is a fertile lowland region of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a> and Bridgend, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> Channel coast. After the Norman conquest of Glamorgan around 1093 its manors were granted to the knights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-fitzhamon">Robert Fitzhamon</a>, who built castles and churches there, while Welsh lords kept the uplands. It is now a county borough.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vale-of-glamorgan.jpg" alt="Vale of Glamorgan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeremy Segrott from Cardiff, Wales, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nash_Point,_Vale_of_Glamorgan,_south_Wales_(26088435901).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Vale of Glamorgan lies immediately west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a> between the M4 motorway and the Severn Estuary. The county borough covers 33,097 hectares and has 53 kilometres of coastline. The district that gives it its name is slightly larger and generally low-lying, extending from the River Kenfig in the west to the Rhymney in the east, with the Pennant Sandstone scarp forming its northern boundary. The highest point is Tair Onen, east of Cowbridge, at 137.3 metres. The yellow-grey cliffs of the Glamorgan Heritage Coast, between Gileston and Ogmore-by-Sea, are formed of Liassic limestone, shale and Carboniferous Limestone and hold traces of Jurassic sea creatures such as ammonites.</p><p>Calcium carbonate in the soil allows crops to be grown that would be difficult elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. In earlier centuries limestone was taken by sloops across the Bristol Channel to North Cornish ports such as Bude, Boscastle and Port Isaac to fertilise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>'s poor slate soils, and hard Devonian slate came back as a roofing material. The coast faces west and takes the brunt of the Atlantic gales, which created ideal conditions for deliberate shipwrecking along it. Nash Point, Southerndown and Ogmore-by-Sea have some of the highest numbers of shipwreck victims on the Welsh coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between the 11th century and 1536 the area was part of the Lordship of Glamorgan. The village of Cosmeston, near Penarth, grew up around a fortified manor house built about the 12th century by the De Costentin family, who came from the Cotentin peninsula in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and were among the first Norman invaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1066. The village would have consisted of small thatched stone round houses. In 1974 the Vale of Glamorgan Borough was created as a second-tier district of the new county of South Glamorgan, with a population of 103,000, and in April 1996 it became a county borough, a unitary authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Vale of Glamorgan is a county borough in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, bordering Bridgend, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a>, Rhondda Cynon Taf and the Bristol Channel. The largest town is Barry, and others include Penarth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llantwit-major">Llantwit Major</a> and Cowbridge. Its economy rests largely on agriculture and chemicals, with chemical industries east of the port of Barry and beef and dairy farming inland. Sites include the medieval wall paintings in St Cadoc's Church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llancarfan">Llancarfan</a>, St Donat's Castle and Cosmeston Medieval Village. Cardiff Airport lies near Rhoose.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vale of Glamorgan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He granted manors in the fertile <b>Vale of Glamorgan</b> to his knights, while Welsh lords kept the uplands.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-glamorgan">Lordship of Glamorgan</a></li></ul></section>
